). The low of <strong>44\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If New Bern's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, New Bern has set <strong>75 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 108 that chance alone would give, and <strong>93 record highs</strong>, close to the 108 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (60). New Bern's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on July 22, 1952.","t_chg":"New Bern's nights got warmer. Its days didn't.","p_chg":"Across 88 complete years: New Bern's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.10\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.00 to +0.20), about +1.0\u00b0F since 1933. New Bern's average high shows no clear change: +0.01\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.09 to +0.11) that includes zero.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months. Highs rose in none.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"New Bern's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.18 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 53.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (79.17 in); the driest, 1965 (36.07 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 12 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}